ssible strategies of straight, triplet, airplane.  Playing that four-pair straight - 44-55-66-77- is a solid opener.  It gets rid of the most vulnerable cards (the low pairs 4, 5, 7) and sets up a nice hand going forward. I\'m left with the triplet 999, the high pairs KK and AA, and the ace card 2.\n\nThis will either force my opponents to block me with their big guns or they\'ll be left behind with a bunch of single cards. This sets me up to control the flow and win. If they pass, I\'ll hit them with the 999 and the 3 and keep dumping.\n\nThis opens my turn for the high value cards - if the opps are playing singles, the 2 will come in handy - and pairs will be a struggle. But I have 6, 8, Q which is not that bad - not ideal but better than the alternatives. It\'s a strong move that gives me the initiative and sets me up for a win.  The pair straight is harder to block and is likely to win the first round.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand contains multiple pairs (44, 55, 77, KK, AA) and triplets (666, 999). The most problematic cards are the low pairs (44, 55, 77), which are hard to play individually and easily beaten. The most effective strategy is to combine these into a "Pair Straight" (Sequence of Pairs) using the 6s. Playing the 4-pair sequence `44-55-66-77` clears 8 cards at once and is very difficult for opponents to block (requires a sequence like 88-99-1010-JJ or higher). This move breaks the 666 triplet but leaves a very strong backend: a triplet of 9s, high pairs KK and AA, and the 2 for control. The alternative of playing a single straight (3-4-5-6-7-8) leaves the hand fragmented with disconnected singles and pairs, making it much harder to win.\n\nReason: This move maximizes card efficiency by clearing weak low pairs in a strong combination that is hard to counter, retaining high control cards (KK, AA, 2) and a triplet (999) to finish the game.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♦4", "♠5", "♣5", "♥6", "♦6", "♥7", "♣7"]}'}
